      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =((1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(-1-2x(1-x)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =((2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(4x-2-2x(2{x}^{2}-2x-1)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(2{x}_{n}^{2}-2{x}_{n}-1){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}-2){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(2{x}_{0}^{2}-2{x}_{0}-1){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{0}^{3}+4{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}-2){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(2(1.5)^{2}-2(1.5)-1){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}}{(-4(1.5)^{3}+4(1.5)^{2}+6(1.5)-2){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
